WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Space travel. It’s one of humanity’s most truly&nbsp;
miraculous achievements: Building the technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:13.920
to defy gravity and journey into the stars above,&nbsp;
a place our ancestors could only dream of going.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.440
Throughout history, humans have found gods and&nbsp;
heroes in the constellations, and today when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.320
we look up into the sky, we have the privilege&nbsp;
of knowing that flesh-and-blood mortals live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.160
up there alongside them. It’s a humbling,&nbsp;
inspirational, and downright awesome thing.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.760
But, of course, there’s always going to be&nbsp;
naysayers. People who just want to rain on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.200
our interstellar parade. And nobody fits this&nbsp;
bill harder than people affected by an anomalous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:49.040
phenomenon classified by the SCP Foundation as&nbsp;
SCP-2001, the “Space Oddity.” And before you ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.600
no, the late David Bowie is not and never has&nbsp;
been an SCP. To truly understand this anomaly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.720
we need to take a look at three different people&nbsp;
who are affected by it to varying degrees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.560
Let’s start with your friend, Kevin.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.240
Even if you don’t have a friend called&nbsp;
Kevin, you definitely have at least a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.680
casual acquaintance just like him. Kevin is a&nbsp;
very confident man with plenty of ill-informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.400
opinions he’d be more than happy to argue&nbsp;
about until the heat death of the universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.560
He’s the kind of guy who can turn a discussion&nbsp;
about your favorite movie into World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.480
III. But you never knew until recently that&nbsp;
he held such strong opinions on space travel.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.400
One day, you were discussing the latest rocket&nbsp;
launch with a mutual friend of yours while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.480
Kevin just happened to be in earshot. The&nbsp;
second you start talking about how exciting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.240
it’ll be to one day put a person on Mars, he&nbsp;
swaggers over, face twisted into a grimace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.600
You’re already preparing for him&nbsp;
to say something cringe inducing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.120
and Kevin being Kevin, he doesn’t disappoint.
“Ugh, don’t tell me you two dweebs are talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.160
about space travel. What a stupid waste of money.&nbsp;
You guys know there’s nothing but rocks up there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:59.280
right? Aliens don’t exist. It’s all just another&nbsp;
excuse for dumb scientists to waste my tax dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.320
on playing with their stupid toys. How about you&nbsp;
two talk about something that really matters?”

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:11.280
You may be thinking Kevin is just a jerk, and he&nbsp;
definitely is, but he’s also a victim. A victim of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.920
SCP-2001. You see, 2001 is described as an&nbsp;
anomalous series of neural oscillations that occur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:24.720
during three of the four stages of sleep. Namely,&nbsp;
NREM - or “Non-Rapid Eye Movement” - Sleep. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.760
one of these three stages, the amygdala, a part&nbsp;
of the brain also known as “The Lizard Brain”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.120
enters a heightened state of activity for ten to&nbsp;
fifteen minutes. Once the process has started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.720
even waking up the affected individual will&nbsp;
do nothing to stop the actual infection.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.080
There are no traits that predispose people&nbsp;
to SCP-2001 infection. It appears to infect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.680
people in equal measures regardless of age,&nbsp;
gender, and race. Spectrographic analysis on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:57.120
the afflicted has shown that the condition may&nbsp;
not be intraneural - meaning, originating within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.200
the brain - and may actually be the product of&nbsp;
an outside influence. The invariable effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.360
SCP-2001 is the infected developing negative&nbsp;
feelings towards the concept of space travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.960
in increasing gradations of severity depending&nbsp;
on which stage of NREM sleep the anomaly began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.040
to take effect. Of real concern to the Foundation&nbsp;
is that SCP-2001 seems to be manifesting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:27.680
increasing frequency in astronomers, astronauts,&nbsp;
and individuals who otherwise deal with space.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.080
The SCP-2001 case manifests&nbsp;
in three different varieties:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:38.080
Gamma, Beta, and Alpha. Each variety has&nbsp;
its own unique traits and idiosyncrasies.

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.440
We’ve encountered a Gamma type already - your&nbsp;
dear friend, Kevin. This is the most mild type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:50.320
of SCP-2001 infection, and also the most common,&nbsp;
affecting at least 1% of the global population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.960
It occurs for those who were infected during&nbsp;
the second stage of NREM sleep. People with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.800
Gamma-Type SCP-2001 infection will, for the&nbsp;
most part, seem like their normal selves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.840
save for a disparaging attitude towards the&nbsp;
concept of space travel. Whenever it’s brought up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.480
they’ll likely complain and change the subject&nbsp;
immediately, but it won’t affect their behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.800
too much overall. The same cannot be said&nbsp;
for people with Beta-Type SCP-2001 infection.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.000
Meet Riley. You’ve never spoken to Riley before,&nbsp;
but she’s a young woman who likes to hang out at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.000
your local cafe, often seen frantically typing on&nbsp;
her laptop. You assume she’s writing a screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.360
or a novel, but in fact, she’s posting&nbsp;
simultaneously on multiple conspiracy forums.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.640
Among her favorite theories are that the earth is&nbsp;
flat, space is a hologram, and the moon landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.440
was faked by Stanley Kubrick, director of such&nbsp;
films as The Shining and, appropriately, 2001:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.800
A Space Odyssey. You made the heinous mistake of&nbsp;
going to your local cafe at the same time as Riley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.400
one morning, while wearing a NASA baseball cap.&nbsp;
An error that you would come to regret deeply.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.960
You bought a latte and decided to sit down&nbsp;
for a moment, just the briefest little rest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.840
but that was all the time Riley needed.&nbsp;
Her eyes seemed to burn with hatred.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.440
You asked if there was anything you&nbsp;
could do to help her, and she responded.&nbsp;

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.600
“You’re just buying into the propaganda. Do&nbsp;
you have any idea what NASA really stands for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.200
It’s ‘Never A Straight Answer.’ All their&nbsp;
supposed achievements - the moon landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.480
the ISS, dehydrated astronaut food - it’s all a&nbsp;
big sham! NASA is just a slush fund for corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.600
government officials, no human has ever been able&nbsp;
to survive in space. It’s too dangerous - anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:32.400
sent up there will die immediately, no exceptions.&nbsp;
And even if they did it would be pointless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:36.960
space is just a dark, infinite void that goes on&nbsp;
forever. It’s just a big pile of dangerous lies!”

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.680
Feeling awkward, you decide to just get up and&nbsp;
leave. That’s when Riley grabs your NASA cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.520
right off of your head and throws it in&nbsp;
the trash. That’s fine, you don’t intend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.200
on going back for it. You just keep walking,&nbsp;
trying your best to move calmly out the door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.840
Wise decision, because Riley is&nbsp;
actually a Beta-Type SCP-2001&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.680
infectee. As you can probably tell, Beta&nbsp;
cases are a lot more severe than Gamma cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.560
and people infected with them seem to come off&nbsp;
as deranged conspiracy theorists with a rabid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.160
hatred of all space travel. They’ll form elaborate&nbsp;
fantasies about the falsification of space travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.200
and will often decry it as too pointless&nbsp;
or dangerous to ever be undertaken.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:25.360
But of course, the danger of Beta-Types pale&nbsp;
in comparison to Alpha-Types. These are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:31.600
infected by SCP-2001 during REM sleep, the stage&nbsp;
of sleep typically associated with dreaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:37.520
As a result, its effects on the mind are far&nbsp;
more dangerous and profound. Take, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:43.120
the tragic case of SCP Foundation Junior&nbsp;
Researcher Martinez. He was one of the many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.560
operatives stationed at Foundation Outpost-12,&nbsp;
a complex that works in coordination with NASA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:54.080
to launch both manned and unmanned missions into&nbsp;
space on behalf of the Foundation’s interests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:59.760
It’s a valuable asset to the Foundation in&nbsp;
investigating and combating space-based anomalies.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.200
Martinez, who had previously spent time&nbsp;
working with SCP-2001 infected D-Classes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.760
was part of ground control, something that&nbsp;
would prove to be a literally fatal oversight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.320
on the part of the Foundation. During the&nbsp;
early stages of takeoff for a manned craft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:20.160
Martinez seened to become extremely agitated.&nbsp;
Over time this agitation steadily increased,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.720
and eventually became a full-on breakdown&nbsp;
as the rocket ascended. He pulled out some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.480
kind of strange, anomalous control device, and&nbsp;
began disrupting the operations of the rocket.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.520
Martinez was shot dead by&nbsp;
guards as a defensive measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:39.360
but it was already too late. The craft exploded,&nbsp;
and everyone on board was tragically killed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.000
Later investigations into the incident found a&nbsp;
crucial piece of evidence tying Junior Researcher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.400
Martinez to SCP-2001. They discovered a journal&nbsp;
entry he’d written shortly before his breakdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.640
which seemed to have been written in a&nbsp;
completely different style to prior entries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:58.640
implying that some kind of massive&nbsp;
psychological shift had occurred.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:01.440
The following is the contents&nbsp;
of that journal entry.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.240
”I wish to state first that my mind&nbsp;
and body are perfectly sound. I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:11.120
prepared to submit myself to any and&nbsp;
all tests to prove that I'm clean.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.400
I have the answer to SCP-2001.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:17.920
Just yesterday, during an interview&nbsp;
with an Alpha-infected D-Class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.400
I was asked a question that stopped my mind for&nbsp;
a minute. The woman I was interviewing asked me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:28.480
if I knew the Foundation was right. I said no, I&nbsp;
wasn't sure. Then she asked me, what if we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.240
right. I could only assume that by "we", she meant&nbsp;
2001 carriers. I said nothing for a while, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:39.600
then she spoke. She told me of a dream she'd had a&nbsp;
while ago. She said that something talked to her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.720
told her about how dangerous outer space was. It&nbsp;
showed her the beasts that roam the void between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.677
stars, about the fractal beings that absorb the&nbsp;
very fabric of reality, about the… this part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.677 --> 00:08:55.280
of the entry was expunged… that we're keeping&nbsp;
in that godforsaken door. And she was scared.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.440
She told me that at the end of the dream,&nbsp;
as she woke up, it told her what it was.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:00.960
It's all of space.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.880
Space fears for us. We're living, breathing&nbsp;
lifeforms, and space loves us and cherishes us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:12.160
She asked me if I had any idea what the chances&nbsp;
of intelligent life forming on another planet are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.640
then she told me a number that took a&nbsp;
minute and a half to recite. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:20.880
I couldn't speak. I started thinking:&nbsp;
about the demographics of infection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:27.120
about the symptoms, about more expunged data&nbsp;
here... and suddenly, it all made sense. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.560
of the things she said stuck in my brain, and&nbsp;
they all added up to the same thing: that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:37.120
are in grave danger if we go out there. We can't&nbsp;
go out there. We can't go out there. We can't.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:43.760
There's a reason behind SCP-2001, and it's nowhere&nbsp;
near as terrifying as all of my colleagues think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.720
Space wants to protect us. There are things&nbsp;
out there that would wipe humanity clean off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:53.760
of this plane of existence, and we are&nbsp;
too precious to our mother, to our host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.360
to the universe we live in, for&nbsp;
her to allow that to happen.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.400
What I am going to do tomorrow&nbsp;
is not a result of infection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.920
It is not a result of madness. It is not a result&nbsp;
of anything other than purest knowledge. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.920
I've found out the truth. And it's more&nbsp;
beautiful than I ever could have dreamed.”

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.680
Given what Martinez would later go on to do, it’s&nbsp;
clear that he wasn’t in as sound a state of mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.840
as he thought. As you’ve probably already&nbsp;
gathered, people suffering from Alpha-Type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:26.560
SCP-2001 infection will do anything they can to&nbsp;
actively prevent or sabotage attempts at space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:32.400
travel. This is a minor concern when it comes to&nbsp;
civilians, but when it infects people who actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.080
work in these fields - like Junior Researcher&nbsp;
Martinez - it can have devastating results.

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:42.960
But this isn’t where this story ends.&nbsp;
While it’s currently extremely classified,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:48.800
there is actually a fourth category of SCP-2001&nbsp;
infection, discovered in the wake of Martinez’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.920
death. A key element of the Alpha strain is that&nbsp;
it cannot cause people to perform acts that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.520
would find immoral, such as murdering the entire&nbsp;
crew of a manned shuttle with a crash. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.880
the fourth category - known as Alpha-Prime&nbsp;
- does away with any moral compunctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:11.360
whatsoever. People infected with this virus will&nbsp;
prevent space travel by any means necessary.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:17.440
And unlike other SCP-2001 infections, you&nbsp;
aren’t compromised in your sleep this time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:22.800
The vector for infection is simply talking to&nbsp;
someone infected with the regular Alpha strain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.760
and hearing a few key trigger phrases. That’s&nbsp;
how Junior Researcher Martinez caught it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:33.120
from the D-Classes he was working with. What&nbsp;
are these key phrases? We still don’t know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.560
But if you find that you suddenly&nbsp;
have much stronger opinions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:41.040
space travel than you’ve ever had&nbsp;
before, try to keep them to yourself.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:45.200
Now check out “SCP-096 - Looking at&nbsp;
a picture of the Shy Guy in space?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.240
SCP-096 Questions and Theories”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.000
and “SCP-5000 - Why? The Full Compilation”&nbsp;
for more fascinating Foundation facts!

